begin model

#@title: COVID-19 vaccination and variants, Houston MSA (Mallela et al., 2024)

#@description: |
#  Deterministic compartmental model of COVID-19 transmission dynamics in the
#  metropolitan statistical area (MSA) surrounding Houston (Houston-The
#  Woodlands-Sugar Land, TX), parameterized to daily reports of newly confirmed
#  cases from 2020-01-21 to 2021-10-31. It extends the model of Lin et al.
#  (2021) with vaccination and two more transmissible SARS-CoV-2 variants.
#  Unvaccinated persons progress S -> E1..E5 (a five-stage Erlang incubation
#  period) -> A (asymptomatic) or I (mildly symptomatic) -> H (severe) -> R /
#  D, each carrying a behavioral state M (mixing) <-> P (social-distancing) or
#  Q (quarantined by testing or symptom-driven self-isolation). Vaccination
#  moves susceptible and recovered persons at the empirical per capita rate
#  mu(t) into V1..V6, a six-stage Erlang waiting time for vaccine-induced
#  immunity; persons leaving V6 are partitioned into SV,1..SV,4 by how many of
#  the three circulating strains their immune response neutralizes, and
#  breakthrough infections run through a single-stage incubation EV -> AV / IV
#  -> HV -> R / D. Social distancing is two coordinated step functions, a
#  setpoint protected fraction Ptau(t) and an eigenvalue Lambdatau(t) setting
#  how fast that setpoint is approached, which switch at sigma, tau1, tau2,
#  tau3; a third step function Ytheta(t) multiplies the transmission rate
#  constant beta by y1 at the Alpha takeoff time theta1 and by y2 at the Delta
#  takeoff time theta2. A counter() molecule integrates simulation time into
#  the observable t, which every if(t...) rate law reads. Behavior: 4 distinct
#  social-distancing periods plus the two variant step changes reproduce the
#  observed sequence of surges in detected cases over 2020-2021 -- the fit
#  observable fDCs_Cum is the cumulative count of detected symptomatic cases,
#  whose day-to-day increment is the predicted daily case report. Parameters
#  hold the authors' published maximum a posteriori estimate.

#@keyword: |
#  epidemiology, COVID-19, SARS-CoV-2, vaccination, viral variant, Alpha
#  variant, Delta variant, compartmental model, SEIR, social distancing,
#  quarantine, Erlang, mass action, step function, Bayesian inference,
#  Homo sapiens

#@reference: |
#  Mallela A, Chen Y, Lin YT, Miller EF, Neumann J, He Z, Nelson KE, Posner RG,
#  Hlavacek WS (2024). Impacts of Vaccination and Severe Acute Respiratory
#  Syndrome Coronavirus 2 Variants Alpha and Delta on Coronavirus Disease 2019
#  Transmission Dynamics in Four Metropolitan Areas of the United States.
#  Bull Math Biol 86:31. doi:10.1007/s11538-024-01258-4 -- the model
#  (Appendix Eqs. 1-40), the fixed parameters (Table 3), and the Houston
#  maximum a posteriori estimate (Table 1) reproduced here (Fig. 3A).
#  Lin YT, Neumann J, Miller EF, Posner RG, Mallela A, Safta C, Ray J, Thakur G,
#  Chinthavali S, Hlavacek WS (2021). Daily Forecasting of Regional Epidemics of
#  Coronavirus Disease with Bayesian Uncertainty Quantification, United States.
#  Emerg Infect Dis 27(3):767-778. doi:10.3201/eid2703.203364 -- the base model
#  that this one extends, and the source of the fixed epidemiological constants.

#@note: |
#  PROVENANCE. Molecule types, seed species, functions, and rules follow the
#  authors' own PyBioNetFit model (Houston/Houston.bngl in the Vax_and_Variants
#  example of lanl/PyBNF), house-styled here: component names lowercased,
#  switch-time parameters re-expressed in the paper's notation (sigma, tau1,
#  tau2, tau3, theta1, theta2 are absolute times built from the fitted
#  increments t_sigma, t_tau1, t_tau2, t_tau3, t_theta2, which is how the
#  authors parameterized the fit and what keeps the switch times ordered),
#  fH/25 written as the paper's mh*fH, and the observable set reduced to those
#  the rate laws and the paper's Fig. 3C population decomposition need.
#  Fitted-parameter nominals are the published MAP
#  (Houston/Output/adaptive_files/MLE_params.txt), which agrees with Table 1 of
#  Mallela et al. (2024) to the precision printed there.
#
#  VACCINATION GATE. v_times_PhiV() carries the authors' `if(t<=582,...)` gate
#  verbatim: no vaccination occurs before day 583 (2021-08-26). For Houston the
#  gate is inert: the empirical series (Democrat and Chronicle) itself starts
#  on day 584, with the earlier vaccinations entering as a lump sum on day
#  600-601. This is the model as fit -- the published MAP was inferred through
#  the gate, and it is what reproduces the reported case data; releasing it
#  (mu(t) active from the first reported day) suppresses the 2021 Delta surge
#  and degrades agreement with the case data. See verify_mallela2024.ipynb.
#
#  UNITS. Populations are counts of individuals; the model is inherently
#  population-based (no molar concentration), so no NA / reference-volume
#  conversion applies. The negative-binomial dispersion parameter r of the
#  likelihood (Table 1) is not a model parameter and is omitted here.

begin parameters

  # === Region-specific fixed parameter; see Table 3 in Mallela et al. (2024) ===
  # Total population of the Houston (Houston-The Woodlands-Sugar Land, TX) MSA
  S0 7066141 # individuals

  # === Adjustable parameters at the published MAP; see Table 1 in Mallela et al. (2024) ===
  # Start of local disease transmission; model day 0 = 2020-01-21
  t0 11.23749021723255 # d
  # Delay from t0 to the start of the initial social-distancing period
  t_sigma 57.60387717514558 # d
  # Start of the initial social-distancing period
  sigma t0+t_sigma # d
  # Delay from sigma to the start of the 2nd social-distancing period
  t_tau1 93.31665619745147 # d
  # Start of the 2nd social-distancing period
  tau1 sigma+t_tau1 # d
  # Delay from tau1 to the start of the 3rd social-distancing period
  t_tau2 23.72699768421979 # d
  # Start of the 3rd social-distancing period
  tau2 tau1+t_tau2 # d
  # Delay from tau2 to the start of the 4th social-distancing period
  t_tau3 92.23675057112717 # d
  # Start of the 4th social-distancing period
  tau3 tau2+t_tau3 # d

  # Setpoint fraction of susceptibles practicing social distancing, one per
  # period (p0, p1, p2, p3)
  p0 0.2892400715142916 # dimensionless
  p1 0.3859179796183396 # dimensionless
  p2 0.3372556004995734 # dimensionless
  p3 0.1742600349194912 # dimensionless

  # Social-distancing eigenvalues setting how fast that setpoint is approached
  # (lambda0, lambda1, lambda2, lambda3)
  lambda0 7.465434865144207 # /d
  lambda1 0.11347092594892 # /d
  lambda2 0.5313499163805552 # /d
  lambda3 3.239689007703006 # /d

  # Takeoff time of SARS-CoV-2 variant Alpha (lineage B.1.1.7)
  theta1 400.1793882188741 # d
  # Delay from the Alpha takeoff to the Delta takeoff
  t_theta2 120.4067576233636 # d
  # Takeoff time of SARS-CoV-2 variant Delta (lineage B.1.617.2)
  theta2 theta1+t_theta2 # d
  # Transmissibility of Alpha relative to ancestral strains
  y1 1.192215797420976 # dimensionless
  # Transmissibility of Delta relative to ancestral strains
  y2 1.982867192891535 # dimensionless
  # Rate constant for disease transmission by ancestral strains
  beta 0.2869901012881623 # /d
  # Fraction of new symptomatic infections detected and reported
  fD 0.3592935046766061 # dimensionless

  # === Universal fixed parameters; see Table 3 in Mallela et al. (2024) ===
  # Number of infectious persons at t = t0
  I0 1 # individuals
  # Reduction in risk of infection from social distancing
  mb 0.1 # dimensionless
  # Reduction in risk of severe disease from vaccination
  mh 0.04 # dimensionless
  # Relative infectiousness of persons in the incubation period
  rhoE 1.1 # dimensionless
  # Relative infectiousness of asymptomatic persons
  rhoA 0.9 # dimensionless
  # Fraction of infections that never become symptomatic
  fA 0.44 # dimensionless
  # Fraction of symptomatic cases that become severe (unvaccinated)
  fH 0.054 # dimensionless
  # Fraction of persons with severe disease who recover
  fR 0.79 # dimensionless
  # Cumulative fraction of vaccinated persons protected against ancestral
  # strains (f0), against Alpha as well (f1), and against Delta as well (f2)
  f0 0.9 # dimensionless
  f1 0.81 # dimensionless
  f2 0.69 # dimensionless
  # Rate constant for progression through each incubation stage
  kL 0.94 # /d
  # Rate constant for testing-driven quarantine
  kQ 0.0038 # /d
  # Rate constant for symptom-driven self-isolation
  jQ 0.4 # /d
  # Rate constant for completing immune clearance without symptoms
  cA 0.26 # /d
  # Rate constant for completing immune clearance or progressing to severe disease
  cI 0.12 # /d
  # Rate constant for recovery from or death after severe disease
  cH 0.17 # /d
  # Rate constant for progression through each stage of the vaccine response
  kV 0.3 # /d

  # === Bookkeeping ===
  # Case-reporting period (delta_t = 1 => daily case reports)
  delta_t 1 # d
  # Social-distancing periods considered beyond the initial one; see Table 1
  n 3 # dimensionless

end parameters

begin molecule types

  # Simulation clock: 0->counter() at rate 1 integrates time; observable t = counter
  counter()
  # Susceptible and unvaccinated; sd~M = mixing, sd~P = social-distancing
  S(sd~M~P)
  # Vaccinated and unexposed, by the breadth of the immune response raised
  #  1: no strain neutralized, 2: ancestral only, 3: ancestral + Alpha,
  #  4: ancestral + Alpha + Delta
  SV(strain~1~2~3~4)
  # Incubation period; stage~1..5 are the Erlang stages for unvaccinated persons
  #  (vax~1 uses a single exponential stage), sd~Q = quarantined
  E(stage~1~2~3~4~5,sd~M~P~Q,vax~0~1)
  # Asymptomatic and in the immune-clearance phase
  A(sd~M~P~Q,vax~0~1)
  # Mildly symptomatic and in the immune-clearance phase
  I(sd~M~P~Q,vax~0~1)
  # Vaccinated; stage~1..6 are the Erlang stages of the immune response
  V(stage~1~2~3~4~5~6)
  # Severe disease; hospitalized or isolated at home
  H(vax~0~1)
  # Recovered
  R(vax~0~1)
  # Deceased
  D()
  # Detected-case accumulator (the integral of fD times the detection rate)
  fDCs()

end molecule types

begin seed species

  counter() 0 # dimensionless
  S(sd~M) S0-I0 # individuals
  I(sd~M,vax~0) I0 # individuals
  fDCs() 0 # individuals

end seed species

begin observables

  # Simulation clock read by every if(t...) rate law
  Molecules t counter()

  # Susceptible and unvaccinated, by behavioral state (S_M, S_P)
  Molecules S_M S(sd~M)
  Molecules S_P S(sd~P)

  # Exposed and unvaccinated, all five incubation stages (vaccination-eligible)
  Molecules E_M E(sd~M,vax~0)
  Molecules E_P E(sd~P,vax~0)
  # Exposed, unvaccinated and infectious (stages 2-5; stage 1 is not infectious)
  Molecules Einf_M E(stage~2,sd~M,vax~0) E(stage~3,sd~M,vax~0) \
    E(stage~4,sd~M,vax~0) E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0)
  Molecules Einf_P E(stage~2,sd~P,vax~0) E(stage~3,sd~P,vax~0) \
    E(stage~4,sd~P,vax~0) E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0)
  # Exposed and vaccinated (E_V); the whole population is infectious
  Molecules E_V E(vax~1)
  # Last incubation stage, unvaccinated: the source of newly symptomatic cases
  Molecules E5_MPQ E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0) E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0) \
    E(stage~5,sd~Q,vax~0)
  # Exposed and quarantined, all stages (informational)
  Molecules E_Q E(sd~Q,vax~0)

  # Asymptomatic, immune-clearance phase
  Molecules A_M A(sd~M,vax~0)
  Molecules A_P A(sd~P,vax~0)
  Molecules A_Q A(sd~Q,vax~0)
  Molecules A_V A(vax~1)
  # Mildly symptomatic, immune-clearance phase
  Molecules I_M I(sd~M,vax~0)
  Molecules I_P I(sd~P,vax~0)
  Molecules I_Q I(sd~Q,vax~0)
  Molecules I_V I(vax~1)

  # Vaccinated and awaiting immunity (V1..V6), lumped
  Molecules V_tot V()
  # Vaccinated and unexposed, by breadth of protection (SV,1..SV,4)
  Molecules SV_1 SV(strain~1)
  Molecules SV_2 SV(strain~2)
  Molecules SV_3 SV(strain~3)
  Molecules SV_4 SV(strain~4)

  # Severe disease, recovered, and deceased
  Molecules H_U H(vax~0)
  Molecules H_V H(vax~1)
  Molecules R_U R(vax~0)
  Molecules R_V R(vax~1)
  Molecules D_tot D()

  # Fit observable: cumulative detected symptomatic cases, fD*C_S(t)
  Molecules fDCs_Cum fDCs()

end observables

begin functions

  # Empirical daily per capita vaccination rate mu-tilde(t); see Eq. 37 in
  # Mallela et al. (2024). A step function holding the reported fraction of the
  # Houston MSA population completing vaccination on each day after
  # 2020-01-21 (Democrat and Chronicle); days 584-649 carry data, all others are 0.
  v_rate()=\
    if(t<=583,0,if(t<=584,0.00285410171,if(t<=585,0.00181851251,if(t<=586,0,if(t<=587,0,\
    if(t<=588,7.81945674e-06,if(t<=589,0.0260988707,if(t<=590,1.20699099e-05,\
    if(t<=591,4.84130246e-05,if(t<=592,1.20699099e-05,if(t<=593,0,\
    if(t<=594,5.62324813e-05,if(t<=595,4.25045319e-06,if(t<=596,0,\
    if(t<=597,4.84130246e-05,if(t<=598,0.0454863859,if(t<=599,0.0272833862,\
    if(t<=600,0.371058367,if(t<=601,0.0751567757,if(t<=602,0.00080481444,\
    if(t<=603,0.000152975476,if(t<=604,0.000933000791,if(t<=605,0.000719988529,\
    if(t<=606,0.000214861876,if(t<=607,0.000725878136,if(t<=608,0.000127349329,\
    if(t<=609,0.00079968162,if(t<=610,0.000163276237,if(t<=611,0.000832159976,\
    if(t<=612,5.29768116e-05,if(t<=613,0.000747170969,if(t<=614,0.000151439195,\
    if(t<=615,5.4615965e-05,if(t<=616,0.000841054682,if(t<=617,0.000146875408,\
    if(t<=618,0.000673214658,if(t<=619,0.000249252504,if(t<=620,0.000151439195,\
    if(t<=621,0.00138700025,if(t<=622,0.000803716784,if(t<=623,0.000223778989,\
    if(t<=624,0.000730441923,if(t<=625,9.84623832e-05,if(t<=626,0.00079394484,\
    if(t<=627,0.000235779128,if(t<=628,0.000861046922,if(t<=629,0.000109209293,\
    if(t<=630,0.000144842685,if(t<=631,0.000838107412,if(t<=632,4.84130246e-05,\
    if(t<=633,0.000685284568,if(t<=634,0.000219508718,if(t<=635,4.25045319e-06,\
    if(t<=636,0.000260976705,if(t<=637,0.000248162371,if(t<=638,0.00149362281,\
    if(t<=639,0.000435533363,if(t<=640,0.00088246254,if(t<=641,0.00104872636,\
    if(t<=642,0.000916264221,if(t<=643,0.00109030719,if(t<=644,0.00103540322,\
    if(t<=645,0.000920514675,if(t<=646,0.00105438028,if(t<=647,0.0009734914859999999,\
    if(t<=648,0.000939517065,if(t<=649,0.000902941083,\
    0)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))

  # Population eligible for vaccination, phi_V(t); see Eq. 31
  PhiV()=if(t>=t0,S_M+S_P+E_M+E_P+A_M+A_P+R_U,0)

  # Per capita vaccination rate mu(t) = S0*mu-tilde(t)/phi_V(t). The t<=582 gate
  # is the authors' own; see the VACCINATION GATE note in the header.
  v_times_PhiV()=if(t<=582,0,S0*v_rate()/PhiV())

  # Variant transmissibility factor Y_theta(t); see Eq. 38
  Ytheta()=if(t<=theta1,1,if(t<=theta2,y1,y2))

  # Unit step functions U_sigma, U_theta1, U_theta2; see Eqs. 32-34
  Usigma()=if(t>=sigma,1,0)
  Utheta1()=if(t>=theta1,1,0)
  Utheta2()=if(t>=theta2,1,0)

  # Quarantine, self-isolation, and immune-clearance rate constants are held at
  # 0 until the local epidemic starts, so I stays at I0 for t < t0
  kQfunc()=if(t>=t0,kQ,0)
  jQfunc()=if(t>=t0,jQ,0)
  cIfunc()=if(t>=t0,cI,0)

  # Effective infectious populations phi_M(t) and phi_P(t); see Eqs. 29-30
  phiM()=if(t>=t0,I_M+I_V+rhoE*(Einf_M+E_V)+rhoA*(A_M+A_V),0)
  phiP()=if(t>=t0,I_P+rhoE*Einf_P+rhoA*A_P,0)

  # Social-distancing eigenvalue Lambda_tau(t) and setpoint P_tau(t), the two
  # coordinated step functions of Eqs. 35-36; both are 0 before t = sigma
  Lambdatau()=\
    if(t>=sigma && t<tau1,lambda0,\
    if(t>=tau1 && t<tau2,lambda1,\
    if(t>=tau2 && t<tau3,lambda2,\
    if(t>=tau3,lambda3,\
    0))))
  Ptau()=\
    if(t>=sigma && t<tau1,p0,\
    if(t>=tau1 && t<tau2,p1,\
    if(t>=tau2 && t<tau3,p2,\
    if(t>=tau3,p3,\
    0))))

end functions

begin reaction rules

  # Simulation clock and the detected-case accumulator; see Eqs. 39-40
  0->counter() 1
  0->fDCs() fD*(1-fA)*(kL*E5_MPQ+kL/5*E_V)

  # Disease transmission to unvaccinated susceptibles; see Eqs. 1-4
  S(sd~M)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~0) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  S(sd~P)->E(stage~1,sd~P,vax~0) mb*(Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())

  # Adoption and relaxation of social distancing (mixing <-> protected)
  S(sd~M)<->S(sd~P) Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),\
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~0)<->E(stage~1,sd~P,vax~0) \
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  E(stage~2,sd~M,vax~0)<->E(stage~2,sd~P,vax~0) \
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  E(stage~3,sd~M,vax~0)<->E(stage~3,sd~P,vax~0) \
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  E(stage~4,sd~M,vax~0)<->E(stage~4,sd~P,vax~0) \
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0)<->E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0) \
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  A(sd~M,vax~0)<->A(sd~P,vax~0) Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),\
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())
  I(sd~M,vax~0)<->I(sd~P,vax~0) Usigma()*Lambdatau()*Ptau(),\
    Usigma()*Lambdatau()*(1-Ptau())

  # Testing-driven quarantine of exposed persons (stage 1 is undetectable)
  E(stage~2,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~2,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~3,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~3,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~4,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~4,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~5,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~2,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~2,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~3,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~3,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~4,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~4,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ
  E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~5,sd~Q,vax~0) kQ

  # Progression through the five stages of the incubation period
  E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~2,sd~M,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~2,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~3,sd~M,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~3,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~4,sd~M,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~4,sd~M,vax~0)->E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~1,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~2,sd~P,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~2,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~3,sd~P,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~3,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~4,sd~P,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~4,sd~P,vax~0)->E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~2,sd~Q,vax~0)->E(stage~3,sd~Q,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~3,sd~Q,vax~0)->E(stage~4,sd~Q,vax~0) kL
  E(stage~4,sd~Q,vax~0)->E(stage~5,sd~Q,vax~0) kL

  # Entry into the immune-clearance phase, without and with symptoms
  E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0)->A(sd~M,vax~0) kL*fA
  E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0)->A(sd~P,vax~0) kL*fA
  E(stage~5,sd~Q,vax~0)->A(sd~Q,vax~0) kL*fA
  E(stage~5,sd~M,vax~0)->I(sd~M,vax~0) kL*(1-fA)
  E(stage~5,sd~P,vax~0)->I(sd~P,vax~0) kL*(1-fA)
  E(stage~5,sd~Q,vax~0)->I(sd~Q,vax~0) kL*(1-fA)

  # Quarantine and symptom-driven self-isolation in the immune-clearance phase
  I(sd~M,vax~0)->I(sd~Q,vax~0) jQfunc()+kQfunc()
  I(sd~P,vax~0)->I(sd~Q,vax~0) jQfunc()+kQfunc()
  A(sd~M,vax~0)->A(sd~Q,vax~0) kQfunc()
  A(sd~P,vax~0)->A(sd~Q,vax~0) kQfunc()

  # Recovery, progression to severe disease, and death (unvaccinated)
  A(vax~0)->R(vax~0) cA
  I(vax~0)->R(vax~0) cIfunc()*(1-fH)
  I(vax~0)->H(vax~0) cIfunc()*fH
  H(vax~0)->R(vax~0) cH*fR
  H(vax~0)->D() cH*(1-fR)

  # Vaccination of susceptible and of recovered unvaccinated persons at the
  # empirical per capita rate mu(t); see Eqs. 17-18 and 28
  S(sd~M)->V(stage~1) v_times_PhiV()
  S(sd~P)->V(stage~1) v_times_PhiV()
  R(vax~0)->R(vax~1) v_times_PhiV()

  # Progression through the six stages of the immune response to vaccination
  V(stage~1)->V(stage~2) kV
  V(stage~2)->V(stage~3) kV
  V(stage~3)->V(stage~4) kV
  V(stage~4)->V(stage~5) kV
  V(stage~5)->V(stage~6) kV

  # Persons leaving V6 partition by how many circulating strains they neutralize
  V(stage~6)->SV(strain~1) kV*(1-f0)
  V(stage~6)->SV(strain~2) kV*(f0-f1)
  V(stage~6)->SV(strain~3) kV*(f1-f2)
  V(stage~6)->SV(strain~4) kV*f2

  # Breakthrough infection of vaccinated persons; those still awaiting immunity
  # (V1..V6) are fully susceptible, SV,2 only from the Alpha takeoff time and
  # SV,3 only from the Delta takeoff time; see Eqs. 18-22 and 24
  V(stage~1)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  V(stage~2)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  V(stage~3)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  V(stage~4)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  V(stage~5)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  V(stage~6)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  SV(strain~1)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) (Ytheta()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  SV(strain~2)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) \
    (Ytheta()*Utheta1()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  SV(strain~3)->E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1) \
    (Ytheta()*Utheta2()*beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())

  # Immune clearance after breakthrough infection; vaccination lowers the risk
  # of severe disease by mh but not the probability of death once severe
  E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1)->A(sd~M,vax~1) (kL/5)*fA
  E(stage~1,sd~M,vax~1)->I(sd~M,vax~1) (kL/5)*(1-fA)
  A(sd~M,vax~1)->R(vax~1) cA
  I(sd~M,vax~1)->R(vax~1) cIfunc()*(1-mh*fH)
  I(sd~M,vax~1)->H(vax~1) cIfunc()*(mh*fH)
  H(vax~1)->R(vax~1) cH*fR
  H(vax~1)->D() cH*(1-fR)

end reaction rules

end model

begin actions

  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 42 species, 88 reactions

  #@note: |
  #  ODE-only. Time gating uses the counter() molecule as a deterministic clock
  #  (0->counter() at rate 1, so the observable t equals simulation time); this
  #  and the if(t...) rate-law functions require deterministic integration --
  #  under SSA the clock would be a Poisson process, and NFsim supports neither
  #  if() rate laws nor a population this size (7,066,141 individuals).

  # Houston MSA daily new-case reproduction

  #@protocol: |
  #  Integrate the compartmental ODE over 2020-01-21 to 2021-10-31 (model day
  #  t = 0..649) at the published maximum a posteriori estimate. The fit
  #  observable fDCs_Cum is the cumulative count of detected symptomatic cases;
  #  its day-to-day increment is the predicted number of cases reported that
  #  day, which verify_mallela2024.ipynb overlays on the New York Times
  #  surveillance data. Expect 4 distinct social-distancing periods (Ptau
  #  and Lambdatau step at sigma, tau1, tau2, tau3) and two variant
  #  step changes: Ytheta rises to y1 = 1.2 on day 400 (Alpha) and to
  #  y2 = 2 on day 521 (Delta), which together reproduce the
  #  observed sequence of surges in detected cases.

  #@figure: Fig. 3 in Mallela et al. (2024)

  simulate({method=>"ode",suffix=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>649,n_steps=>649,\
    print_functions=>1,atol=>1e-7,rtol=>1e-7})

end actions
